:answer.type/math :answer/value "p=-3,\\text{ }q=-13" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3bab717f-01cb-58ed-b0d5-b19f53406b98" :answer-field/id #uuid "3bab717f-01cb-58ed-b0d5-b19f53406b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["db54cc2a-edfc-550f-8892-f5aa690b9b72" "6c18d946-6e69-50be-87ed-92fd19d56764" "eff54709-4354-528a-a040-8a400ddfcf46" "f3aa1d31-a517-54f2-8aab-3cd7c0f31ddc" "5a9065e2-37e9-598b-a708-7db1109a4c02"] :answer-field/correct "5a9065e2-37e9-598b-a708-7db1109a4c02" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "ff0f8a51-4ab6-507a-8e84-b31ad82bcba0" :question/id #uuid "ff0f8a51-4ab6-507a-8e84-b31ad82bcba0" :question/problem "If the expression ${z}^{2}-6z-4$ is written as $(z+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z-4\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-4$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}-2\\cdot 3z+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z-3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z-3{)}^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=-13.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["3bab717f-01cb-58ed-b0d5-b19f53406b98"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "fa59444f-70cc-54c9-963b-6ffc26dce375" :answer/id #uuid "fa59444f-70cc-54c9-963b-6ffc26dce375" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=-158" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "dbd2faa6-dc9f-58d6-a12d-3c8ee3d8b9af" :answer/id #uuid "dbd2faa6-dc9f-58d6-a12d-3c8ee3d8b9af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=-63" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "2555a0a9-b99b-520a-9ac6-c67eeaf8fcb6" :answer/id #uuid "2555a0a9-b99b-520a-9ac6-c67eeaf8fcb6" :answer/type :a